/*
======================================================================
card_row_temporal — STAGE 3. PER-ROW temporal features, computed in GSQL.
======================================================================
Six backward-looking features per TRANSACTION, from one card-anchored
gather -> sort -> single forward scan.

======================================================================
NO build_id. NO cutoff_event_seq. THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN.
======================================================================
Every value below is a function of that card's events STRICTLY EARLIER
than the row itself. The reference time is always the ROW'S OWN
unix_time, exclusive of the row. Such a feature is causal BY
CONSTRUCTION and is therefore valid for every build simultaneously --
so this query runs ONCE, like target_edges, not once per build.

That is not a shortcut, it is the leak-safe choice, and the alternative
is actively wrong. A temporal feature anchored at the BUILD CUTOFF
("transactions in the 7 days before this snapshot") has its magnitude
bounded by (cutoff time - data start), so the column ENCODES WHICH
SNAPSHOT SERVED THE ROW. It also inherits the fold geometry: each
training fold serves a ~4.9M-rank window while b_val and b_test serve
~1.3M each, so the scored splits would get temporal features several
times fresher than the fitted ones. A row-anchored window has no cutoff
in it at all, and both problems vanish.

It is also the right SHAPE. systemprompt records that
Card.max_travel_velocity_kmh and Card.impossible_travel_count were
DELETED for exactly this reason: "Both were CARD-level aggregates while
the label is per-transaction." The shipped replacement
(pair_home_distance_km) is row-level. A per-entity temporal scalar
stamped onto every one of a card's transactions repeats a mistake this
repo already diagnosed and paid for.

======================================================================
WHAT IS EMITTED, AND WHICH SENTINEL EACH ONE USES
======================================================================
  row_card_gap_seconds      this row's unix_time minus the card's
                            previous transaction. -1 on a card's FIRST
                            transaction: there is no previous one, so
                            the gap does not exist.
  row_pair_gap_seconds      same, but the card's previous transaction
                            AT THIS MERCHANT. -1 on first visit. This
                            is what answers Story 5b's "per merchant"
                            without a merchant-anchored pass -- see the
                            refusal below.
  row_card_txn_count_1h     prior transactions in [t-3600, t)
  row_card_txn_count_24h    prior transactions in [t-86400, t)
  row_card_amount_24h       sum of their amounts
  row_card_tenure_seconds   this row's unix_time minus the card's
                            EARLIEST transaction

THE SENTINEL SPLIT IS DELIBERATE AND FOLLOWS wcc_bipartite's cc_degree
REASONING. The two gaps use -1 because on a first transaction they are
UNDEFINED. The counts, the amount and the tenure are 0 on a first
transaction and 0 is the TRUE, KNOWABLE answer -- zero prior
transactions, zero prior spend, zero elapsed tenure. Coding those as -1
would throw away a real observation and make "no history" indistinguish-
able from "not computed". Only register the two gaps in
SENTINEL_AT_MINUS_ONE.

======================================================================
MERCHANT-ANCHORED PER-ROW FEATURES ARE REFUSED, NOT DEFERRED
======================================================================
The gather-sort idiom materialises one vertex's entire history in a
vertex-attached accumulator, and batching by getvid residue CANNOT
split a single hot vertex. On the regenerated load one merchant reaches
51% of all cards. A merchant-anchored gather would therefore put a
double-digit-percent share of the corpus into one accumulator, which is
the exact shape that produced the System Memory abort on 2026-08-02.
row_pair_gap_seconds is per-(card, merchant) and comes free off the
card-side gather, which is the useful part of "per merchant" anyway.

======================================================================
ONE HOP, NOT TWO
======================================================================
Payment_Transaction carries FLAT join keys -- card_number and
merchant_id (schema.gsql:423-424) -- so the merchant is read off the
transaction record and Transaction_To_Merchant is never traversed. Half
the expansion of card_interval_max, which walks the second hop only to
learn the same merchant id.

Cost: one 1-hop expansion over the transaction edges plus an O(n log n)
sort per card, ONCE for the whole run rather than once per build.

======================================================================
num_of_source_batches IS SIZED BY ROWS, NOT BY CARDS
======================================================================
Every batch returns one output row per transaction it covers, and the
whole query returns one row per transaction in the corpus. The binding
constraint is therefore SERIALISATION, not graph work: match the fact
exporter's page size (tigergraph_export.fact_page_rows) rather than the
card-partition counts used by the build queries. Batching by getvid
residue does not balance rows exactly -- cards differ in activity -- so
leave headroom.

heap_capacity must exceed the BUSIEST card's transaction count. The
failure direction is benign: HeapAccum(cap, ut ASC) keeps the smallest
on overflow, so truncation DROPS trailing rows, which the row-count
reconciliation catches loudly rather than silently understating a
value. cards_truncated_MUST_BE_ZERO gates it anyway.
======================================================================
*/

USE GRAPH TF_GNN

CREATE OR REPLACE QUERY card_row_temporal(
    INT source_batch = 0,
    INT num_of_source_batches = 1,
    INT heap_capacity = 5000
) FOR GRAPH TF_GNN SYNTAX V2 {

  TYPEDEF TUPLE<UINT ut, UINT eseq, DOUBLE amt, STRING tid, STRING mid> TxnPoint;

  TYPEDEF TUPLE<STRING id, UINT event_seq,
                DOUBLE row_card_gap_seconds,
                DOUBLE row_pair_gap_seconds,
                INT    row_card_txn_count_1h,
                INT    row_card_txn_count_24h,
                DOUBLE row_card_amount_24h,
                DOUBLE row_card_tenure_seconds,
                DOUBLE row_card_gap_ratio,
                DOUBLE row_amount_z,
                INT    row_pair_txn_count> TemporalRow;

  HeapAccum<TxnPoint>(heap_capacity, ut ASC) @heap;
  ListAccum<TxnPoint>                        @sorted;

  /* Last time this card was seen at each merchant, updated AS the scan
     advances so it only ever holds strictly-earlier rows. MaxAccum<UINT>
     defaults to 0 and a real unix_time is never 0, so get() == 0 means
     "not seen yet" -- no containsKey needed. */
  MapAccum<STRING, MaxAccum<UINT>> @last_seen;
  /* Prior visits to each merchant, incremented AFTER the emit like @last_seen.
     Gives row_pair_txn_count: depth of the relationship, where
     row_pair_gap_seconds gives its recency. */
  MapAccum<STRING, SumAccum<INT>>  @pair_count;
  SumAccum<INT>                    @txn_total;

  ListAccum<TemporalRow> @@rows;
  SumAccum<INT> @@rows_emitted, @@cards_truncated, @@cards_with_history;

  INT W1  = 3600;
  INT W24 = 86400;

  card_batch = SELECT c FROM Card:c
               WHERE getvid(c) % num_of_source_batches == source_batch;

  /* event_seq > 0 excludes the unknown sentinel. NO cutoff: the window
     is bounded by the row, not by a build. */
  gather = SELECT c
      FROM card_batch:c -(Card_Send_Transaction>:e1)- Payment_Transaction:t
      WHERE t.event_seq > 0
      ACCUM c.@heap += TxnPoint(t.unix_time, t.event_seq, t.amount,
                                t.id, t.merchant_id),
            c.@txn_total += 1;

  scan = SELECT c FROM card_batch:c
      POST-ACCUM
        IF c.@txn_total > heap_capacity THEN @@cards_truncated += 1 END,

        /* ut ASC, so popping yields ascending time. */
        WHILE c.@heap.size() > 0 DO
            c.@sorted += c.@heap.pop()
        END,

        INT n = c.@sorted.size(),
        INT lo1 = 0,
        INT lo24 = 0,
        DOUBLE amt24 = 0,
        DOUBLE gap = -1,
        DOUBLE pgap = -1,
        UINT seen_at = 0,
        /* Running moments over PRIOR rows only, advanced after each emit.
           gap_sum/gap_n give the mean prior gap; amt_sum/amt_sq give the mean
           and variance of prior amounts. All three new features are functions
           of these, so they cost no extra traversal. */
        DOUBLE gap_sum = 0,
        DOUBLE gap_n = 0,
        DOUBLE amt_sum = 0,
        DOUBLE amt_sq = 0,
        DOUBLE ratio = -1,
        DOUBLE zscore = -1,
        DOUBLE mean_amt = 0,
        DOUBLE var_amt = 0,

        IF n > 0 THEN
            @@cards_with_history += 1,

            FOREACH hi IN RANGE[0, n-1] DO
                /* Both windows cover PRIOR rows only -- indices
                   [lo, hi-1]. lo stops at hi, which is an empty window,
                   so it never passes hi and never indexes past the end.
                   Exclude when (t - ut) > W, i.e. keep ut >= t - W. */
                WHILE lo24 < hi AND
                      (c.@sorted.get(hi).ut - c.@sorted.get(lo24).ut) > W24 DO
                    amt24 = amt24 - c.@sorted.get(lo24).amt,
                    lo24 = lo24 + 1
                END,
                WHILE lo1 < hi AND
                      (c.@sorted.get(hi).ut - c.@sorted.get(lo1).ut) > W1 DO
                    lo1 = lo1 + 1
                END,

                gap = -1,
                IF hi > 0 THEN
                    gap = c.@sorted.get(hi).ut - c.@sorted.get(hi-1).ut
                END,

                seen_at = c.@last_seen.get(c.@sorted.get(hi).mid),
                pgap = -1,
                IF seen_at > 0 THEN
                    pgap = c.@sorted.get(hi).ut - seen_at
                END,

                /* NORMALISED velocity. A card that transacts hourly and one
                   that transacts monthly are not comparable on raw gap; they
                   are on gap over its own mean. -1 until a mean exists, which
                   needs two prior rows. */
                ratio = -1,
                IF gap_n > 0 AND gap > 0 THEN
                    ratio = gap / (gap_sum / gap_n)
                END,

                /* "Unusually large FOR THIS CARD". amount is absolute today,
                   which cannot express that. Population variance over priors;
                   needs two priors for a non-zero denominator. */
                zscore = -1,
                IF gap_n > 1 THEN
                    mean_amt = amt_sum / (gap_n + 1),
                    var_amt = amt_sq / (gap_n + 1) - mean_amt * mean_amt,
                    IF var_amt > 0 THEN
                        zscore = (c.@sorted.get(hi).amt - mean_amt)
                                 / sqrt(var_amt)
                    END
                END,

                @@rows += TemporalRow(
                    c.@sorted.get(hi).tid,
                    c.@sorted.get(hi).eseq,
                    gap,
                    pgap,
                    hi - lo1,
                    hi - lo24,
                    amt24,
                    c.@sorted.get(hi).ut - c.@sorted.get(0).ut,
                    ratio,
                    zscore,
                    c.@pair_count.get(c.@sorted.get(hi).mid)
                ),
                @@rows_emitted += 1,

                /* THIS row becomes a prior for the next one. Both updates
                   happen AFTER the emit, which is what keeps the window
                   strictly earlier than the row it describes. */
                c.@last_seen += (c.@sorted.get(hi).mid -> c.@sorted.get(hi).ut),
                c.@pair_count += (c.@sorted.get(hi).mid -> 1),
                amt24 = amt24 + c.@sorted.get(hi).amt,
                IF gap > 0 THEN gap_sum = gap_sum + gap, gap_n = gap_n + 1 END,
                amt_sum = amt_sum + c.@sorted.get(hi).amt,
                amt_sq = amt_sq + c.@sorted.get(hi).amt * c.@sorted.get(hi).amt
            END
        END,

        c.@heap.clear(), c.@sorted.clear(),
        c.@last_seen.clear(), c.@pair_count.clear();

  PRINT @@rows AS temporal_rows;

  PRINT @@rows_emitted       AS rows_returned,
        @@cards_with_history AS cards_with_history,
        @@cards_truncated    AS cards_truncated_MUST_BE_ZERO,
        source_batch         AS source_batch,
        num_of_source_batches AS source_batch_count;
}
